  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Labour Administration Systems in the Caribbean are strengthened</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Individual and collective labour disputes are inevitable in employment relationships, and well-designed institutions are essential for harmonious industrial relations and labour market governance. The ILO recognizes that improving access to labour justice requires revising regulatory frameworks, streamlining procedures, reducing costs, and enhancing the qualifications and capacity of personnel in courts and dispute resolution institutions. To address these needs, the ILO plans to organize a subregional symposium on labour dispute prevention and resolution, aiming to foster an exchange of experiences and knowledge among heads of dispute resolution institutions in the Caribbean.

Beneficiaries of this initiative include workers, employers, and the broader community in the Caribbean, who will benefit from more effective and accessible labour dispute resolution mechanisms. The work will be done in collaboration with judicial and non-judicial institutions, including conciliation, mediation, and arbitration services. The ILO will provide financial and technical support through the Regional Office for Latin America and the Caribbean, promoting social dialogue and labour relations based on international standards. This effort aims to establish a network of heads of dispute resolution institutions and develop evidence-based policies to address the root causes of labour conflicts.</narrative>
  </description>
